# Cursor Rules for Figma Plugin Development

## Key Resources
- Figma Plugin API: https://www.figma.com/plugin-docs/intro/
  - Use `figma` global object, JavaScript for logic, HTML/CSS for UI.
  - Handle async operations (e.g., `figma.loadFontAsync`).
- Plugin Review Guidelines: https://help.figma.com/hc/en-us/articles/360047285394
  - No crashes, accurate metadata, support contact in UI.
  - Avoid non-API external packages.
- Community Guidelines: https://help.figma.com/hc/en-us/articles/360039958634
  - No trademark misuse or offensive content.
- Standards of Conduct: https://www.figma.com/standards-of-conduct/
  - Secure data handling (e.g., OAuth2.0), legal compliance.
- Security: https://forum.figma.com/t/security-considerations-when-deploying-plugin/39182
  - Secure OAuth2.0, safe fileKey handling.
- MCP Server: https://github.com/figma/mcp-server
  - Run `npx fig2ai <figma-design-url>` for `.designrules`.

## Coding Standards
- Language: JavaScript (logic), HTML/CSS (UI).
- API: Use `figma` object for interactions.
- UI: Render via `figma.showUI`, handle `figma.ui.onmessage`.
- Security: Secure OAuth2.0, data handling.
- Testing: Error handling, test for crashes.
- Dependencies: No non-API external packages.
- Metadata: Accurate name, description, support contact.

## Cursor Instructions
- Generate code compliant with above resources.
- Validate against Plugin Review Guidelines.
- Use `.designrules` from MCP server for design context.
- Audit security (OAuth2.0, fileKey).
- Align UI/metadata with Brand/Community Guidelines.

## Figma
- Before you claim something it finished, make sure you verify compatibility with Figma's environment

### JavaScript Compatibility Requirements for Figma Plugins
Here's an exhaustive list of JavaScript compatibility limitations in the Figma plugin environment:

#### Syntax Limitations
- No ES Modules - Cannot use import/export syntax or ES modules
- No CommonJS Modules - Cannot use require() statements
- No Optional Chaining - Cannot use ?. syntax (e.g., obj?.prop?.method())
- No Nullish Coalescing - Cannot use ?? operator
- No Spread Syntax - Cannot use ... for objects or arrays (e.g., {...obj} or [...array])
- No Object/Array Destructuring with Default Values - Basic destructuring works, but not with defaults
- Limited Template Literals - Basic template literals work, but not with expressions in some contexts
- No Dynamic Imports - Cannot use import() function
- No Private Class Fields - Cannot use #privateField syntax
- No Class Static Initialization Blocks - Cannot use static {} blocks
- No BigInt Literals - Cannot use 123n syntax
- No Numeric Separators - Cannot use 1_000_000 syntax
- No Logical Assignment Operators - Cannot use ||=, &&=, or ??=
- No Top-level Await - Cannot use await outside of async functions

#### API and Runtime Limitations
- No DOM API - Cannot access document, window, or DOM APIs directly
- No Browser APIs - No access to localStorage, sessionStorage, indexedDB, etc.
- Limited Timer Functions - setTimeout/setInterval work but with limitations
- No Web Workers - Cannot use Web Workers
- No Fetch API - Must use figma.ui.postMessage for communication
- No WebSockets - No direct WebSocket support
- Limited Console Methods - Some console methods may not work as expected
- No Blob/File APIs - Limited support for binary data handling
- No URL/URLSearchParams - Limited URL parsing capabilities
- No Intl API - Limited internationalization support
- No Proxy Objects - Cannot use Proxy for object interception
- No Shared Array Buffers - Limited shared memory capabilities
- No Service Workers - Cannot use service workers
- No Geolocation API - No access to user location
- No Web Audio API - Limited audio capabilities
- No WebGL/Canvas API - Cannot directly render graphics
- No Clipboard API - Limited clipboard access

#### Best Practices for Figma Plugin Development
- Use ES5 Syntax - Stick to ES5 syntax for maximum compatibility
- Avoid Arrow Functions in critical code paths
- Use Function Declarations instead of expressions where possible
- Use var instead of let/const in critical code
- Use Object.assign instead of spread syntax
- Use Array.prototype methods instead of spread for arrays
- Use traditional for loops instead of for...of where performance matters
- Use explicit property access instead of optional chaining
- Use if checks instead of nullish coalescing
- Use simple string concatenation instead of complex template literals
- Polyfill missing features when needed
- Test thoroughly in the Figma plugin environment
- Use figma.clientStorage instead of localStorage
- Use figma.ui.postMessage for UI communication
- Handle async operations with proper error handling

#### Figma-Specific Limitations
- No direct file system access - Must use Figma APIs for file operations
- Limited network requests - Must use UI iframe for network requests
- No direct access to user's clipboard - Must use Figma APIs
- No direct access to system fonts - Must use figma.loadFontAsync()
- Limited plugin size - Keep plugin code compact
- No direct access to the main thread - UI runs in a separate context
- No direct manipulation of Figma UI - Must use Figma APIs
- Limited plugin persistence - Use clientStorage for data persistence
- No background processing - Plugin only runs when active
- Limited access to user's system - No file system or OS integration